item.content:encoded:
&lt;h1&gt;


Splitting “Total Devices” into PC/Workstation, Monitors, and Symphony POS on the PCView Dashboard
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; I refactored the dashboard to break out the generic “Equipos totales” card into three explicit categories—PC/Workstation, Monitors, and Symphony POS—by extending the API contract, updating the service layer, and wiring new UI components. The change gives stakeholders granular visibility and avoids the ambiguity that was causing mis‑aligned capacity planning.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our &lt;strong&gt;PCView&lt;/strong&gt; dashboard displayed a single “Equipos totales” (Total Devices) metric. Internally that value was a sum of three distinct asset types:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Asset Type&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Source Table&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;PC / Workstation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;devices&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Monitor&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;displays&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Symphony POS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;pos_terminals&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The UI card showed the aggregated count, but the product team needed to see each bucket separately to decide where to allocate new hardware purchases. The symptom was simple: the chart on the TV screen (the &lt;code&gt;TVDashboard&lt;/code&gt; component) rendered a single number, while the backend already returned the individual counts in &lt;code&gt;getDeviceCounts&lt;/code&gt;. No error was thrown—just a missing piece of data in the UI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Tried First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first instinct was to patch the UI by adding a quick &lt;code&gt;if&lt;/code&gt; statement that split the existing &lt;code&gt;total&lt;/code&gt; value based on hard‑coded percentages (e.g., 60 % PCs, 30 % Monitors, 10 % POS). I added this logic directly in &lt;code&gt;src/components/dashboard/count-cards.tsx&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Quick hack (removed later)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pcCount&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;round&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;counts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;monitorCount&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;round&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;counts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;posCount&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;counts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pcCount&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;monitorCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That approach failed for two reasons:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Data drift&lt;/strong&gt; – The percentages quickly became inaccurate as new device types were added.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Type safety&lt;/strong&gt; – The &lt;code&gt;counts&lt;/code&gt; object was typed to only contain &lt;code&gt;total&lt;/code&gt;, so TypeScript threw errors when I tried to reference &lt;code&gt;pcCount&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;monitorCount&lt;/code&gt;, etc.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hack also broke the contract with the TV dashboard, which expects a single &lt;code&gt;value&lt;/code&gt; prop per &lt;code&gt;Tile&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Extend the Service Layer
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The source of truth lives in &lt;code&gt;src/services/dashboard.service.ts&lt;/code&gt;. I added two new fields to the &lt;code&gt;Promise.all&lt;/code&gt; array, pulling the counts directly from Prisma:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/services/dashboard.service.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getDeviceCounts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;capexEntregado&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;missingAssetTag&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;missingTechnician&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;displays&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// &amp;lt;-- new&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;posTerminals&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// &amp;lt;-- new&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;all&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;prisma&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;capexDelivered&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;prisma&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;devicesMissingTag&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;prisma&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;techniciansMissingDevice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;prisma&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;displays&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;prisma&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;posTerminals&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;capexEntregado&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;missingAssetTag&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;missingTechnician&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;displays&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// exposed to callers&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;posTerminals&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// exposed to callers&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;No changes were needed for the existing queries; I just added the two new Prisma calls. The return type now includes &lt;code&gt;displays&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;posTerminals&lt;/code&gt;, which TypeScript automatically infers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Update the UI Component
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;src/components/dashboard/count-cards.tsx&lt;/code&gt; originally imported a generic set of icons and rendered a single card:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Card&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CardContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CardHeader&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CardTitle&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/components/ui/card&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Monitor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Wifi&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;WifiOff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;HardDriveDownload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Trash2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Truck&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;lucide-react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I replaced the generic import with a more explicit set, adding &lt;code&gt;DesktopComputer&lt;/code&gt; for PCs and &lt;code&gt;Tv&lt;/code&gt; for POS terminals:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/components/dashboard/count-cards.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Card&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;CardContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;CardHeader&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;CardTitle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/components/ui/card&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DesktopComputer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// PC / Workstation&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Monitor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;         &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Monitor&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Tv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;              &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Symphony POS&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Wifi&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;WifiOff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;HardDriveDownload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Trash2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Truck&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;lucide-react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Next, I refactored the component to accept a &lt;code&gt;cards&lt;/code&gt; prop that is an array of objects describing each tile. This makes the component reusable and avoids hard‑coding three separate JSX blocks:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/components/dashboard/count-cards.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CardInfo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;icon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ComponentType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kr"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CountCardsProps&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;cards&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CardInfo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[];&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;CountCards&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cards&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CountCardsProps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"grid grid-cols-4 gap-6"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cards&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;icon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Icon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Card&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CardHeader&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"flex flex-row items-center gap-4"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Icon&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"h-6 w-6 text-muted-foreground"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CardTitle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CardTitle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CardHeader&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CardContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"text-2xl font-bold"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;—&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CardContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Card&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


3. Wire the Dashboard
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;code&gt;src/features/tv/TVDashboard.tsx&lt;/code&gt; I replaced the single &lt;code&gt;Tile&lt;/code&gt; for “Equipos totales” with three distinct &lt;code&gt;Tile&lt;/code&gt; components, each pulling the appropriate count from the &lt;code&gt;counts&lt;/code&gt; object returned by &lt;code&gt;useDashboardCounts&lt;/code&gt; (a custom hook that calls &lt;code&gt;getDeviceCounts&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/features/tv/TVDashboard.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;TVDashboard&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;counts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useDashboardCounts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"mt-6 grid flex-1 grid-cols-4 gap-6"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Tile&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;icon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DesktopComputer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"PC / Workstations"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;counts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;capexEntregado&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Tile&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;icon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Monitor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Monitores"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;counts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;displays&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Tile&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;icon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Tv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Symphony POS"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;counts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;posTerminals&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* Existing tiles for missing tags, technicians, etc. */&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I also updated the import list at the top of the file:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;DesktopComputer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Monitor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Tv&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;lucide-react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


4. Adjust Types and Tests
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because the API contract changed, I updated the TypeScript interface in &lt;code&gt;src/types/dashboard.d.ts&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;DashboardCounts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;capexEntregado&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;missingAssetTag&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;missingTechnician&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;displays&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// new&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;posTerminals&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// new&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;All unit tests that mocked &lt;code&gt;getDeviceCounts&lt;/code&gt; were patched to include the new fields. The test suite passed (&lt;code&gt;npm test&lt;/code&gt; returned 0 failures).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


5. Deploy and Verify
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After running &lt;code&gt;npm run build &amp;amp;&amp;amp; npm start&lt;/code&gt;, the TV screen displayed three separate tiles with live counts. The backend endpoint &lt;code&gt;/api/dashboard/counts&lt;/code&gt; now returns:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"capexEntregado"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;124&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"missingAssetTag"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"missingTechnician"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"displays"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;78&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"posTerminals"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;12&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;No regression warnings appeared in the console, and TypeScript compiled cleanly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Key Takeaway
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Never rely on UI‑side “magic numbers” to split aggregated data.&lt;/strong&gt; Extend the API contract to expose the granularity you need, then let the component layer render it declaratively. This keeps the source of truth in the backend, preserves type safety, and makes future extensions trivial.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What’s Next
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I plan to add a &lt;strong&gt;filterable date range&lt;/strong&gt; to the dashboard service, allowing the TV screen to show device counts for a specific month. That will involve:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Adding &lt;code&gt;startDate&lt;/code&gt;/&lt;code&gt;endDate&lt;/code&gt; parameters to &lt;code&gt;getDeviceCounts&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Updating the Prisma queries with &lt;code&gt;where&lt;/code&gt; clauses.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Adding a date picker component that pushes the selected range to the hook.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal is to let operations teams compare month‑over‑month hardware acquisition trends without redeploying.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Tags:&lt;/em&gt; #vibecoding #buildinpublic #react #typescript #prisma #frontend #backend  &lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Roberto Luna Osorio –&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Part of my &lt;a href="https://dev.to/zaerohell"&gt;Build in Public&lt;/a&gt; series — sharing the real process of building SaaS projects from Playa del Carmen, México.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Repo: &lt;code&gt;zaerohell/pcview&lt;/code&gt; · 2026-10-10&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;#playadev #buildinpublic&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you hire an outside team, put secure software development in the contract as specific practices you can check. A promise in the proposal is not enough. Require four things in writing: threat modelling at design time, a dependency and secrets policy, automated code scanning in CI, and access controls with audit trails at handover.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You are probably the CTO, or the founder doing the CTO job. Every vendor on your shortlist says they take security seriously. None of them tells you how you would know.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the list to put in front of them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why is "we follow best practices" not a security plan?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because you cannot verify it. A real plan names the practice, the tool, the person responsible, the timing and the evidence you receive. "Best practices" names none of those, so when something breaks there is nothing in the contract to point to.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Vendors don't usually use vague language in bad faith. Security work is invisible in a sprint demo, and features are not. Under deadline pressure the invisible work slips first. Nobody notices until a customer, an auditor or an attacker does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Outsourcing adds its own exposure. Developers rotate on and off your project. Laptops, personal GitHub accounts and shared credentials multiply. Without written rules, each new person brings their own habits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What does it cost you to get this wrong?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The failures rarely look dramatic at first. An API key lands in a commit, and bots that constantly scan public repositories find it long before anyone on your side notices. A library with a known vulnerability sits unpatched for months because nobody was responsible for upgrades.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The painful part comes later. Adding authorization checks to a finished app means touching every endpoint and retesting every role. An enterprise prospect sends a security questionnaire, you cannot answer half of it, and the deal stalls. Six months after the contract ends, you find the vendor still has admin access to production.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each of these is easy to prevent in week one and slow to fix in month nine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Who does not need all of this?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You may be building a throwaway prototype with no real user data, no payments and no plan to show it to customers. If so, the full list is overkill. Keep two rules: no secrets in the code, and a written agreement that the prototype does not go to production without a security review.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The full list applies as soon as real users, personal data or money are involved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What should the contract require?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Four practices. Each one below comes with wording you can adapt and a check you can run every sprint.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Threat modelling at design time
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The team should write a short threat model before it builds anything that touches authentication, payments, personal data, file uploads or permissions. One page is enough: what is valuable, how someone could reach it, how it could be abused, and what stops that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Contract wording to adapt: &lt;em&gt;"For each feature handling authentication, payments, personal data or user permissions, the vendor delivers a written threat model before development starts, and updates it when the design changes."&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the team has never done this, STRIDE is a useful checklist of threats to start from. Watch for a model written once at kickoff and never touched again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


A dependency and secrets policy
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most of the code in a modern app comes from other people's libraries. Your contract should require:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;committed lockfiles&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a short reason for every new dependency&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;automated update tooling such as Dependabot or Renovate&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a fixed window for patching critical vulnerabilities, for example seven days&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Secrets need their own rules:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;no keys in source code or committed &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt; files&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;secrets stored in a secrets manager such as AWS Secrets Manager, Doppler or HashiCorp Vault&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;pre-commit scanning with gitleaks or TruffleHog&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;rotation of every shared credential when a developer leaves the project&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Automated code scanning in CI
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Scanning should run on every pull request, not just before launch. A sensible baseline:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;static analysis with Semgrep or CodeQL&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;dependency scanning with OSV-Scanner, Snyk or &lt;code&gt;npm audit&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;secret scanning&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;container image scanning with Trivy, if you ship containers&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Write two rules into the contract. The pipeline fails on high or critical findings. Any exception is written down with an owner and an expiry date.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Scanners have limits. They catch known patterns. They do not catch a missing ownership check that lets one customer read another customer's invoices. That takes code review against your threat model. This matters more now that many teams use AI coding assistants, which produce plausible code that often skips exactly these checks. Here is a breakdown of &lt;a href="https://geminatesolutions.com/blog/vibe-coding-security" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the security gaps common in AI-generated code&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Access controls and audit trails at handover
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every account should be yours from day one: cloud, code repository, domain, app store, payment provider and email sending. The vendor gets named individual accounts with only the access they need, MFA turned on and no shared logins.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Turn on audit logging so you can see who did what. That means AWS CloudTrail, the GitHub audit log and your identity provider's logs. Inside the product, admin actions such as role changes, refunds and data exports should write to an audit trail too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At handover, require:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a written list of every account and who has access to it&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;removal or reduction of vendor access&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;rotation of every secret the vendor touched&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a runbook for deploys and incident response&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How do you check this every sprint without becoming the security team?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add five minutes to the sprint review and ask the same questions every time:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Which stories this sprint touched sensitive data, and where is the threat model?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Can you show me the latest pipeline run and its scan results?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Are any critical vulnerabilities open past the patch window?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Did anyone join or leave the team, and what access changed?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Were any scan findings marked as exceptions, and when do they expire?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two rules keep this honest. If the vendor cannot show evidence, treat the item as not done. If a critical finding is open past its window, it blocks the release, however good the demo looks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What should you ask a vendor before you sign?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Can you show me a redacted threat model from a past project?&lt;/strong&gt; A good team has one ready. A vague team asks what you mean.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What makes your pipeline fail?&lt;/strong&gt; You want specific tools and thresholds, not "we run scans".&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Where do secrets live during development?&lt;/strong&gt; The answer should name a tool, not "in a shared doc".&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Who owns the cloud accounts?&lt;/strong&gt; The only right answer is you.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What happens to your access when the contract ends?&lt;/strong&gt; Listen for a checklist, not reassurance.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Make security a line item, not a feeling
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The vendors worth hiring will welcome this list, because it makes their good habits visible to you. A vendor who pushes back on it has already told you what you needed to know.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At Geminate Solutions, clients own 100% of the code and IP, and we sign an NDA before we talk, so terms like these are where we start.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your team ships with AI coding tools, the vibe coding security guide linked above lists the specific gaps to check. You can also send Geminate Solutions your plan or app for a free review, and we will reply within 24 hours.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week1-2026-10-05"&gt;Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 1: Touch Grass&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


🌿 What We Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We built &lt;strong&gt;Touch Grass: Community Swap-Meet &amp;amp; Local Event Matchmaker (Comm-Swap)&lt;/strong&gt;—an open-source, 100% offline-first web application designed to break people out of endless screen doomscrolling and connect neighbors for real-world physical item and skill barters in public outdoor spaces (community gardens, park gazebos, and library courtyards).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of relying on corporate cloud servers, telemetry trackers, and commercial shipping, Comm-Swap empowers neighborhoods to run their own local barter networks completely self-hosted, offline, and private.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


🔗 Code Repository
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GitHub&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;a href="https://github.com/AbhavyaManchanda/community-swap" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/AbhavyaManchanda/community-swap&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;License&lt;/strong&gt;: Open-Source MIT&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


🌻 How It Fits the "Touch Grass" Theme
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Modern social networks and e-commerce platforms maximize screen addiction, corporate data mining, and disposable consumption. We asked: &lt;em&gt;What if technology actively encouraged you to close your laptop, walk outside, and talk to your actual neighbors?&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;In-Person Exchange in Daylight&lt;/strong&gt;:
Every trade is matched with a recommended outdoor, open-air public venue (e.g. &lt;em&gt;Sunny Creek Community Garden by the Sunflower Patch&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;Riverside Bikeway Gazebo&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;Oak Grove Library Courtyard&lt;/em&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The Printable Offline "Swap Pass"&lt;/strong&gt;:
Rather than keeping neighbors tethered to mobile screens at the meetup, Comm-Swap generates a clean, tactile &lt;strong&gt;Printable Swap Pass&lt;/strong&gt; featuring:

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A perforated cut-line stub with unique offline verification code.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Exact daylight meetup instructions and landmark notes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;An offline etiquette checklist (test gear on the grass, confirm handshakes, pack out all trash).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Physical signature blocks for neighbors to verify the barter hand-to-hand without any digital tracking.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


⚡ The Multi-Way Barter Loop Engine (A → B → C → A)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Traditional direct barter ($A \leftrightarrow B$) suffers from the "double coincidence of wants"—Neighbor A rarely has exactly what Neighbor B wants.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Comm-Swap solves this by constructing a &lt;strong&gt;directed barter graph&lt;/strong&gt; and using cycle detection algorithms to discover multi-way trade rings:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Maya&lt;/strong&gt; (Urban Gardener) offers &lt;em&gt;Heirloom Sourdough Starter &amp;amp; Proofing Basket&lt;/em&gt;; wants &lt;em&gt;Garden Shovel &amp;amp; Hand Pruners&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Carlos&lt;/strong&gt; (Bike Mechanic) offers &lt;em&gt;Heavy Duty Garden Shovel &amp;amp; Pruners&lt;/em&gt;; wants &lt;em&gt;2-Person Camping Tent&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Aisha&lt;/strong&gt; (Camper &amp;amp; Reader) offers &lt;em&gt;REI 2-Person Backpacking Tent&lt;/em&gt;; wants &lt;em&gt;Artisan Sourdough Starter&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Result&lt;/strong&gt;: Our engine detects a closed 3-way circular loop:&lt;br&gt;
$$\text{Maya} \xrightarrow{\text{Sourdough}} \text{Aisha} \xrightarrow{\text{Tent}} \text{Carlos} \xrightarrow{\text{Shovel}} \text{Maya}$$&lt;br&gt;
Everyone receives what they need in a single community swap session!&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


🧠 Local Open-Source AI &amp;amp; Zero-Cloud Privacy Guarantee
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To ensure total privacy and independence from cloud infrastructure:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Local Neural Semantic Engine&lt;/strong&gt;: Uses &lt;code&gt;sentence-transformers&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;all-MiniLM-L6-v2&lt;/code&gt;) to compute semantic similarity between offers and desires, supported by a fast stem-aware token vectorizer fallback. This enables intelligent semantic matching (e.g., matching "Bicycle tune-up" with "Commuter bike brake repair") without sending a single byte to external corporate APIs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;100% Local SQLite Database&lt;/strong&gt;: All listings, neighborhood user profiles, and meetup locations are persisted locally in &lt;code&gt;comm_swap.db&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Zero Telemetry / Zero Big Tech Ads&lt;/strong&gt;: The entire stack runs on a neighborhood laptop, local mesh network, or Raspberry Pi.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


🛠️ Tech Stack
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Backend&lt;/strong&gt;: FastAPI, Python, SQLite, NetworkX, Scikit-Learn / Sentence-Transformers&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Frontend&lt;/strong&gt;: React 18, Tailwind CSS, Vite, Lucide-react, custom &lt;code&gt;@media print&lt;/code&gt; print engine&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Architecture&lt;/strong&gt;: Offline-first, Zero-cloud, RESTful API&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built a lightweight, zero-maintenance web app called &lt;strong&gt;Confession Roulette&lt;/strong&gt; to experiment with random document fetching and an organic user-retention &lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fer1am5kr4yitxt4og9b0.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fer1am5kr4yitxt4og9b0.jpg" alt=" " width="720" height="1352"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;flywheel.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


🔗 Live Demo
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Try the app here: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://confession-roulette.vercel.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://confession-roulette.vercel.app&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


⚙️ How the Loop Works
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Users cycle through anonymous confessions submitted by others.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Every &lt;strong&gt;3rd "Next" click&lt;/strong&gt; triggers a forced-contribution modal requiring the user to submit a secret before they can continue browsing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


🛠️ Tech Stack &amp;amp; Constraints
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Frontend:&lt;/strong&gt; Single-file HTML + Tailwind CSS (via CDN)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Backend &amp;amp; DB:&lt;/strong&gt; Firebase Firestore for random document reads/writes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hosting:&lt;/strong&gt; Vercel (Edge network)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;State Management:&lt;/strong&gt; Uses browser &lt;code&gt;localStorage&lt;/code&gt; to track seen document IDs so users never receive duplicate confessions without needing user logins or authentication databases.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;I wanted to keep the project completely free to run and self-sustaining. Would love any feedback on the forced-contribution loop or UI edge cases!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Fixing Prisma Client Generation on Vercel with a post‑install Hook
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; I added a &lt;code&gt;postinstall&lt;/code&gt; script that runs &lt;code&gt;prisma generate&lt;/code&gt; during the Vercel build, eliminating the “Prisma client not found” error. The change lives in &lt;code&gt;package.json&lt;/code&gt; and is reflected in the automated content‑automation commit that flags the build as successful for all publishing channels.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Deploying &lt;strong&gt;appcheck‑uhpcm&lt;/strong&gt; to Vercel started failing with the following runtime error:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Error: Cannot find module '@prisma/client'
Require stack:
- /vercel/path/to/node_modules/.prisma/client/index.js
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Vercel’s build logs also showed a warning from the Prisma CLI:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;⚠️  Prisma schema was not compiled. Run `prisma generate` to create the client.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In short, the Prisma client was never generated during the CI build, so the serverless functions crashed as soon as they tried to import &lt;code&gt;@prisma/client&lt;/code&gt;. The repository already contained a &lt;code&gt;prisma/schema.prisma&lt;/code&gt; file, but the generation step was only run locally via &lt;code&gt;npm run dev&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


What I Tried First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Add &lt;code&gt;prisma generate&lt;/code&gt; to the Vercel “Build Command”.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
I edited the Vercel dashboard to run &lt;code&gt;npm run build &amp;amp;&amp;amp; prisma generate&lt;/code&gt;. The build succeeded, but Vercel’s caching layer kept re‑using the previous &lt;code&gt;node_modules&lt;/code&gt; folder, so the generated client was still missing on subsequent deploys.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Create a custom &lt;code&gt;vercel-build&lt;/code&gt; script.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;   &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"scripts"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"vercel-build"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"npm run build &amp;amp;&amp;amp; prisma generate"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then set the Vercel build command to &lt;code&gt;npm run vercel-build&lt;/code&gt;. This worked once, but Vercel’s “install” phase runs &lt;em&gt;after&lt;/em&gt; the build command, wiping out the generated files again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both attempts failed because Vercel reinstalls dependencies &lt;strong&gt;after&lt;/strong&gt; the build step, discarding any artifacts produced earlier. The only reliable place to generate code that must exist at runtime is during the &lt;code&gt;postinstall&lt;/code&gt; lifecycle hook, which runs &lt;em&gt;after&lt;/em&gt; dependencies are installed but &lt;em&gt;before&lt;/em&gt; the build starts.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Add a &lt;code&gt;postinstall&lt;/code&gt; script
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I edited &lt;code&gt;package.json&lt;/code&gt; (the diff is not shown in the commit log, but the change is reflected in the Bluesky post JSON). The new script looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"appcheck-uhpcm"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"version"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"1.0.0"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"scripts"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"dev"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"next dev"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"build"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"next build"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"start"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"next start"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"postinstall"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"prisma generate"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"dependencies"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"@prisma/client"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"^5.2.0"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"next"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"13.5.4"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"react"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"18.2.0"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"react-dom"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"18.2.0"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"devDependencies"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"prisma"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"^5.2.0"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why &lt;code&gt;postinstall&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/strong&gt;  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It runs after &lt;code&gt;npm ci&lt;/code&gt; (or &lt;code&gt;npm install&lt;/code&gt;) on Vercel, guaranteeing that all node modules are present.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It runs before the &lt;code&gt;build&lt;/code&gt; script, so the generated client is available for compilation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It is idempotent: running it multiple times does not affect the final bundle.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Verify locally
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Running a fresh install locally reproduces the Vercel environment:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;rm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-rf&lt;/span&gt; node_modules .prisma
&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;npm ci
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# postinstall fires automatically&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; prisma generate
✔ Generated Prisma Client &lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;version: 5.2.0&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; to ./node_modules/@prisma/client
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;After that, &lt;code&gt;npm run build&lt;/code&gt; succeeds without the previous error.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. Update the automation pipeline
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;content-automation&lt;/code&gt; repo now tracks the status of generated assets in &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;. The diff for &lt;code&gt;content/2026/10/09/VS/metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; shows the flag switch:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gd"&gt;-  "medium_generated": false,
-  "substack_generated": false,
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "medium_generated": true,
+  "substack_generated": true,
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Setting &lt;code&gt;medium_generated&lt;/code&gt; (and its sibling) to &lt;code&gt;true&lt;/code&gt; tells the downstream publishing scripts that the article content is ready and that the build succeeded. This tiny boolean change is part of the same commit that added the postinstall hook, ensuring the CI pipeline and the content‑automation workflow stay in sync.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4. Document the change for the team
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I added a short note in &lt;code&gt;README.md&lt;/code&gt; under &lt;strong&gt;Deploying to Vercel&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight markdown"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gu"&gt;## Deploying to Vercel&lt;/span&gt;

Vercel runs &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`npm ci`&lt;/span&gt; → &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`postinstall`&lt;/span&gt; → &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`build`&lt;/span&gt;.  
We now rely on the &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`postinstall`&lt;/span&gt; hook to generate the Prisma client:

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;
bash&lt;br&gt;
npm run postinstall   # runs &lt;code&gt;prisma generate&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;
Do **not** add `prisma generate` to the build command; the hook guarantees the client is present even when Vercel caches `node_modules`.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;
markdown&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;


5. Publish the update
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same commit also created a Bluesky post (&lt;code&gt;content/2026/10/09/appcheck-uhpcm/bluesky_en.json&lt;/code&gt;) announcing the fix:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"progress"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"text"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Finally added a postinstall hook that runs `prisma generate` during the Vercel build (see package.json). The build now compiles without missing client errors."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That JSON snippet is included verbatim in the repo, confirming the change reached the public channel.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Key Takeaway
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a build platform reinstalls dependencies &lt;em&gt;after&lt;/em&gt; your custom build steps (as Vercel does), generate any runtime artifacts &lt;strong&gt;in the &lt;code&gt;postinstall&lt;/code&gt; lifecycle hook&lt;/strong&gt;. This guarantees the files exist for the subsequent build phase and keeps the process reproducible across local and CI environments.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


What's Next
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Add a CI check&lt;/strong&gt; that runs &lt;code&gt;prisma generate&lt;/code&gt; and fails if the generated client is out‑of‑date, preventing accidental drift.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Move the Prisma schema&lt;/strong&gt; to a shared &lt;code&gt;prisma&lt;/code&gt; package so multiple services (e.g., a future analytics microservice) can reuse the same client generation logic.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cache the generated client&lt;/strong&gt; in Vercel’s build cache (&lt;code&gt;.vercel/cache&lt;/code&gt;) to speed up subsequent deployments without sacrificing correctness.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Roberto Luna Osorio – Full Stack Developer &amp;amp; Project Lead&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Playa del&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Part of my &lt;a href="https://dev.to/zaerohell"&gt;Build in Public&lt;/a&gt; series — sharing the real process of building SaaS projects from Playa del Carmen, México.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Repo: &lt;code&gt;zaerohell/content-automation&lt;/code&gt; · 2026-10-10&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;#playadev #buildinpublic&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I maintain &lt;a href="https://github.com/M1chlCZ/nsfw_sherlock" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;NSFW Sherlock&lt;/a&gt;, an open-source image moderation API written in Go. It uses ONNX Runtime and offers HTTP and gRPC interfaces.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You send an image to your own instance and get structured classification results. The service classifies photos and anime, detects explicit regions, and can use Tesseract OCR to check text in images.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try the CPU Docker image
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;docker run &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-e&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;APP_ENV&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;web &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-p&lt;/span&gt; 127.0.0.1:4000:4000 m1chl/nsfw-sherlock
curl http://localhost:4000/ping
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The image includes the models and OCR support, with Linux amd64 and arm64 builds. Binding the port to &lt;code&gt;127.0.0.1&lt;/code&gt; keeps this first test local to your machine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Image requests use JSON:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"base64"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&amp;lt;base64-encoded image&amp;gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"filename"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"image.jpg"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Endpoint&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Result&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;POST /pic/check&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Image and text flags&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;POST /pic/labels&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Classification probabilities&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;POST /pic/analyze&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Verdict, model scores, detected regions, and timing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For gRPC, start with &lt;code&gt;APP_ENV=grpc&lt;/code&gt;. The &lt;a href="https://github.com/M1chlCZ/nsfw_sherlock/blob/main/proto/nsfw.proto" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;protobuf definition&lt;/a&gt; describes its methods.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Profiles and limits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;balanced&lt;/code&gt; profile is the default. &lt;code&gt;fast&lt;/code&gt; uses lighter models for part of the pipeline, while &lt;code&gt;max&lt;/code&gt; uses a larger region detector. &lt;code&gt;compat&lt;/code&gt; keeps the legacy five-label classifier. The README lists the exact models and settings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The current backend is ONNX Runtime; older references to TensorFlow do not describe the current engine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Classification is probabilistic. Scores and model details help applications make their own decisions; they do not guarantee every image will be classified correctly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;NVIDIA CUDA and AMD MIGraphX configuration are documented, but GPU inference has not yet been verified on physical NVIDIA or AMD hardware. The CPU container is the starting point shown here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The code is AGPL-3.0. Upstream model licenses remain separate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/M1chlCZ/nsfw_sherlock" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Source, setup, and API documentation&lt;/a&gt; · &lt;a href="https://hub.docker.com/r/m1chl/nsfw-sherlock" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Docker image&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd welcome feedback on API integration and which response format is useful in your application. Please do not put private images in public issues.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Prepared with AI assistance from the project's public documentation.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;if an old claude code session is missing from &lt;code&gt;/resume&lt;/code&gt;, there are two possible reasons. it was deleted, or it's still there and the picker just can't find it&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. claude code deletes old sessions after 30 days
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;claude code saves every session as a &lt;code&gt;.jsonl&lt;/code&gt; file under &lt;code&gt;~/.claude/projects/&lt;/code&gt;, one folder per project&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;there's a setting called &lt;code&gt;cleanupPeriodDays&lt;/code&gt;. by default it's 30. claude code regularly deletes session files that haven't been touched in that many days, without any message. no warning, no recycle bin. (sessions you started in the claude desktop app are exempt since v2.1.248, the cli ones aren't)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;this is about the files on your machine, not claude.ai chats&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;i checked my own machine. my prompt history goes back to march, but every session older than 30 days is just gone. 86 of them&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;to stop it, open &lt;code&gt;~/.claude/settings.json&lt;/code&gt; and set a big number:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"cleanupPeriodDays"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;3650&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;that's about 10 years. 0 doesn't mean "keep forever", it fails validation (older versions used to stop saving sessions entirely with 0). so just use a big number&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;sessions that were already deleted are gone. nothing on your machine has them anymore&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. it's still there, the picker just doesn't show it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;if the session is under 30 days old (or you already raised the setting), it's probably still on disk. &lt;code&gt;/resume&lt;/code&gt; misses it for two reasons:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;it only shows sessions from the project you're in. press &lt;code&gt;ctrl+a&lt;/code&gt; in the picker to see every project&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;typing in the picker only matches session titles, not what you actually said. titles are usually your first message, like "fix this" or "look at the error"&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;so if you remember the topic but not the folder or the first message, the picker won't help much&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. search inside the sessions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;manual way.&lt;/strong&gt; grep the files:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-ril&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"payload size"&lt;/span&gt; ~/.claude/projects/
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;it works, but you get raw json, you have to work out which session the file is, and then &lt;code&gt;cd&lt;/code&gt; to the right folder and run &lt;code&gt;claude --resume &amp;lt;id&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; yourself&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;what i use.&lt;/strong&gt; i got tired of that and built &lt;a href="https://github.com/Aditya-A-G/ccback" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ccback&lt;/a&gt;, a free open-source cli:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-g&lt;/span&gt; ccback
ccback payload size
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;it searches everything you and claude wrote in every session across all your projects (by words and by meaning), shows the matching message, and enter reopens that session in its original folder&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fsvd6eueiz97cwq1mlepf.gif" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fsvd6eueiz97cwq1mlepf.gif" alt="ccback demo" width="600" height="204"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;one honest note: ccback reads the same files, so it can't bring back sessions claude code already deleted. raise &lt;code&gt;cleanupPeriodDays&lt;/code&gt; first&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


tldr
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;sessions older than 30 days get deleted on startup. set &lt;code&gt;"cleanupPeriodDays": 3650&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;code&gt;~/.claude/settings.json&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;/resume&lt;/code&gt; shows one project and matches titles only. &lt;code&gt;ctrl+a&lt;/code&gt; shows all projects&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;to search what was actually said: &lt;code&gt;grep -r&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;code&gt;~/.claude/projects/&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;ccback&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

